Packet Desk 0.3.0

Install on Debian, Linux Mint Debian Edition, or Raspberry Pi OS with a
graphical desktop:

    sudo apt install ./packet-desk_0.3.0_all.deb

Open Packet Desk from the application menu or run packet-desk. The same
package uses native Python/Tk and pySerial on Intel/AMD and ARM. For a Pi
running Raspberry Pi OS Lite, a graphical desktop session is needed.

1. Attach the TNC via its computer USB port or a compatible USB-to-RS-232
   adapter and cable. A Pi GPIO UART uses TTL levels and must not be
   connected directly to a true RS-232 port.
2. Select the port. A path under /dev/serial/by-id is useful when available.
   Set the baud and flow control to match the TNC's computer serial port.
   The serial format is 8N1. The app starts at 9600 baud with XON/XOFF.
3. Open the port. The app sends no bytes automatically. For a TNC in
   TERMINAL mode, click Cmd to send the factory command character Ctrl+C.
   If your TNC uses a different command character or is in HOST, KISS,
   transparent mode, or autobaud setup, use its manual to get to cmd:.
4. Confirm cmd: before using Connect, Disconnect, Monitor or Converse.
   Set MYCALL to your licensed callsign in the command console if needed.
   Connect accepts a destination callsign and optional digipeaters.
   In CONVERS mode, the input field sends packet text when you press Enter.
   Return to cmd: before using command shortcuts. Monitor displays the
   text the TNC sends in accordance with its own monitor settings.
5. Start log appends the displayed transcript from that point forward.
6. TNC command help includes searchable indexes for the KPC-3 Plus,
   KPC-9612 Plus and KAM XL. Use List firmware commands to see what your
   connected TNC supports. The printable help file is TNC-Commands.txt
   in this directory.

Use New TNC tab to open another physical serial port. Built-in COM ports
commonly appear as /dev/ttyS0 and /dev/ttyS1. You can type a path manually.

TNC profiles
- Use Rename TNC to name the current tab. Use Profiles > Save current TNC
  to keep that name, serial port, baud, flow control, destination and path.
  Renaming a saved TNC also renames its profile.
- Select a saved profile to open it in a new tab. Open port when ready.
- Profiles and appearance are kept in your Packet Desk configuration folder.
  Profiles capture the computer-side settings. Save a DISPLAY transcript
  separately if you need a record of the TNC firmware settings.

Command files
- Open Command file for the selected TNC. Open or create a UTF-8 text file
  and enter one printable ASCII command per line. Blank and # comment lines
  are skipped. Use Save to keep a reusable copy.
- Delete selection and Clear editor are in the Edit menu and the right-click
  menu. Ctrl+Z undoes either action. Save writes the current editor buffer.
- Use Batch > Check syntax to find stray prompts, status lines and common
  DISPLAY annotations before any command is sent. Batch > Command file syntax
  shows examples for Kantronics firmware 8.2.
- At cmd:, click Send command file. Packet Desk waits for cmd: after each line
  and normally stops on a TNC rejection or a 10-second prompt timeout.
  Batch > Continue after rejected lines waits for cmd: and then sends the
  next command after a rejection. The transcript lists rejected lines.
  Stop sending cancels the unsent lines. Commands already sent may have
  taken effect. Use this for configuration, and issue commands that start
  a connection or switch modes separately.
- Start with harmless queries such as VERSION and MYCALL if you want to test
  a batch before changing settings.

Appearance
Use Appearance to select System, Light, Dark, Warm dark or Custom. Customize
colors lets you start from a preset and set each color using a picker or hex
value. Shaded tabs adds raised, shaded tabs to any palette. Both settings
are remembered the next time Packet Desk opens.
Dark uses charcoal panels, pale green text and an active red tab, following
the XLX480 dashboard's color scheme.

Clipboard: Select transcript text and press Ctrl+C, or right-click for
Copy entire transcript. Ctrl+A selects all. In the input field, Ctrl+V
pastes without sending. Send or Enter transmits what is in the field.
Use the Cmd button to send Ctrl+C to the TNC itself.

If the serial device is owned by group dialout and you get Permission
denied, add your desktop user to that group and log out and back in:

    sudo usermod -aG dialout "$USER"

Each tab provides a terminal session for a separate serial port. This release
implements neither Kantronics
binary HOST mode nor KISS or the original KaGOLD multi-session UI. The
old application and its data are not required for this package. This is
a new program, independent of InterFlex Systems Design and Kantronics.

Kantronics KPC-3 Plus manual:
https://kantronics.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/KPC-3Plus-Manual-RevH.pdf
